<div class="guide-styles" data-zaf-dynamic="article" id="guide-body" lang="en">Welcome to Wowhead's Phase 2 Best in Slot Gear list for Protection Paladin Tank in Wrath of the Lich King Classic. Gear in this guide is primarily obtained from Ulduar. This guide will list the recommended gear for your class and role, containing gear sourced from raids, dungeons, PvP, professions, BoE World drops, and reputations.<h2 class="heading-size-2" id="best-in-slot">Best in Slot Gear for Protection Paladin Tank Phase 2</h2>Gearing in Wrath of the Lich King is mostly about determining what type of survivability you want to err towards. This is to say that, you could stack copious amounts of hard avoidance in the form of dodge and parry and reduce the amount of times you're hit per fight. You could otherwise try to stack health multipliers like armor and stamina which will give you a larger effective health pool (EHP), but you'll take hits more often. This is just to say that you have multiple avenues of how you want to approach your gearing process, and they all come with different strengths and weaknesses. We try to strike a balance between these methods but also chiefly equip the highest quality gear per slot, meaning we have tried to select the highest return on your investment per item slot for Phase 2 raiding. <br/>
<br/>
<a href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/zone=4273/ulduar">
<span class="tinyicontxt">Ulduar</span></a> has seen a <a href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/news/wowhead-wrath-classic-database-updated-item-levels-330095">major gear overhaul</a>, with gear being awarded across the board 6 or 13 iLevels based on the size of the raid, or difficulty rating of the encounter. This means that baseline you can expect all gear in <a href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/zone=4273/ulduar">
<span class="tinyicontxt">Ulduar</span></a> to be at the very least a rough sidegrade to your existing gear or almost always an upgrade. This also means that the gear slots which house 252 iLevel gear (25 man hard modes) are at a premium, bar very few exceptions these pieces will never be superceded in phase 2.<br/>
<br/>Occasionally you'll have to wear an item that is ostensibly worse than something else, but is necessary to match set bonus requirements or achieve certain stat caps like crit immunity, hit cap, and the expertise caps. It's then important to remember that your gear in WotLKC will undergo constant change and you might be expected to be able to swap gear to specialize your performance on any given encounter. Because of this, you will have to adapt your sets to continuously meet these necessary criteria. This also means you are encouraged to hold on to gear as long as you can in order to create as big of a bank as possible to draw from.<br/>
<br/>
<a href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/zone=4273/ulduar">
<span class="tinyicontxt">Ulduar</span></a> is also the first raid to feature an almost complete roster of bosses with creature types that do not fall under the umbrella of <a class="icontiny" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/spell=31866/crusade">
<img loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/spell_holy_crusade.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Crusade</span></a>. This means that, with the exception of <a href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/npc=32871/algalon-the-observer">
<span class="tinyicontxt">Algalon the Observer</span></a>, you may want to spec into <a class="icontiny" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/spell=20224/seals-of-the-pure">
<img loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/ability_thunderbolt.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Seals of the Pure</span></a>, or additional points in <a class="icontiny" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/spell=20177/reckoning">
<img loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/spell_holy_blessingofstrength.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Reckoning</span></a> instead. Otherwise if you feel that you have sufficient threat and DPS is not a concern to you, this is a good opportunity to fill out some utility talents.<ul>
<li class="fa fa-question">
<div data-markup-content-target="1">
<b>
<i>The weapon speed question part 2:</i></b>
<br/>You're now all aware that high weapon damage (slow weapons) bonk harder than low weapon damage (fast weapons) right? Good. Unfortunately, this doesn't change at any point in WotLK and practically every avenue for weapon upgrades will dip into contested territory.<br/>
<br/>When weighing weapon upgrades, a strictly tanking-statted weapon may look enticing, but appearances can be decieving: <br/>
<br/>Defensively, when parry haste is enabled on a boss, a slow weapon will also almost always cancel out any of the avoidance advantage a tanking weapon might have because reducing the amount of times a boss can attack you is functionally the same as dodging, parrying or full-blocking incoming hits. Furthermore, parry hasted hits are intrinsically more difficult to deal with than regular hits due to their nature of huge spike damage. Even when parry haste isn't a factor, as is the case in a lot of Tier 8+ encounters agility has remarkably similar properties to dodge which, being the primary offensive stat on a DPS weapon is generally abundant.<br/>
<br/>Offensively, a slower weapon has a massive leg up on any tanking weapon in terms of damage, DPS being a fairly important metric to some and is admittedly never irrelevant for anyone doing raiding content. To give perspective, a +245  weapon in <a class="q4 icontiny" data-entity="item" data-entity-has-icon="true" data-game="wow" data-type="item" href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/item=45947/serilas-blood-blade-of-invar-one-arm">
<img loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/inv_sword_133.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Serilas, Blood Blade of Invar One-Arm</span></a> (or racial equivalent) is not that different to the same upgrade for most other specs that compete for a main-hand. While time on target is reduced for us due to potential raid mechanics that are unique to the tank(s), the dps gain from awarding a weapon to a protection paladin is remarkably good in the circumstances where your boss uptime is high.<ul></ul>
<div class="pad"></div>A huge portion of a weapons item budget is unsurprisingly dedicated to the offensive stats of any given weapon, this means unless the tanking weapon is significantly ahead in iLevel to cancel out strong amounts of agility or secondary stats that may be beneficial to us, the tanking stats are generally fairly lackluster and overall low in impact. This general offset in how the weapons are developed in tier 8 means that you are very inclined to pick up a DPS weapon if your defense permits it.</div></li></ul>
